# Q: what does a unit a script ordered look like on the map — and what does the trajectory layer
#    show now that the probe can give an order?
#
#   MiVic.Game.exe --emplacement-demo --probe tools/probe/map-trajectory.probe --probe-out artifacts/probe/map-trajectory.txt
#
# The fixture is the one tools/probe/emplacement.probe reads as numbers: an empty clearing with
# three Western vehicles parked on it and a Σοβιετικοί command centre behind them, none of them
# moving and none of them ordered by anything. The `units` at the end of this script is what says
# so — the two vehicles nobody ordered are standing exactly where they started 800 ticks earlier —
# and it is why a picture of one line on this map is a picture of one line.
#
# The reading, in three pictures of the same tank:
#
#   1. the order has landed and the tank has not walked yet (tick 1): a route is drawn and there is
#      nothing behind the unit. The census counts it as neither frozen nor moving — its sampling
#      window is not full yet — which is the honest answer for a unit that has just been told to go;
#   2. four hundred ticks later: the same route ahead of it and the ground it has covered behind
#      it, as a line of marks half a second apart. Those marks are the second reading, and the one
#      a plain line would lose: their spacing is the pace, and this march changes ground under
#      itself. At tick 200 the tank is in deep mud, taking 104 mm a tick at 261 permille of what
#      the hull does on clear ground; by tick 400 it is on sand, taking all 400 of them. A hundred
#      and four millimetres a sample is a mark every two pixels and four hundred is one every
#      eight, which is the difference between a dotted line and a dashed one;
#   3. the end of the march: the goal reached, the route gone with it, and the trail the only thing
#      left of the walk.
#
# A route drawn ahead of a unit and a trail behind it is the picture this whole layer exists for,
# and until `order move` existed it could not be taken from a script at all: the only way to make a
# unit walk was a right-click, and a right-click needs a selection, a camera and a mouse. The order
# below goes through the call that right-click makes — `SimWorld.OrderMove`, see `order` in
# docs/PROBE.md — so the transcript and the picture describe a match rather than a staged world.

# --- the order, given through the call the player's own right-click makes -----------------------
# Slot 508 is the far tank of the fixture, at (x 173, z -192) m: 150 m east of the clearing's
# centre, on mud, facing the middle of the map. (40, -130) m is open ground 146 m away across the
# clearing, far enough from the command centre behind it that arriving is not the start of a
# gunfight — the layer being read here is the trajectory, and a target line across the picture is
# another layer's business.
order 508 move 40 -130

# --- the route, and nothing behind it yet -------------------------------------------------------
tick 1
unit 508
routes
map artifacts/probe/map-order-t1.svg terrain,units,paths,trails,stuck,frame scale=2

# --- the same route, with eighty-eight metres of ground behind it -------------------------------
tick 399
unit 508
routes
map artifacts/probe/map-order-t400.svg terrain,units,paths,trails,stuck,frame scale=2

# --- and the two halves of that layer, apart, at the same tick ----------------------------------
# With only routes on, the tank is a line pointing south-west with nothing behind it; with only
# trails on, it is a dashed line of where it has been with nothing in front of it. Both files are
# of the tick above and carry that tick's census, because a layer turned off is a thing not drawn
# rather than a thing not there.
map artifacts/probe/map-order-paths.svg terrain,units,paths,stuck,frame scale=3
map artifacts/probe/map-order-trails.svg terrain,units,trails,stuck,frame scale=3

# --- the end of the march -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The goal reached and the route gone with it: the layer is a path *and* where the unit has been,
# and a picture holding one of the two says which of them the unit is doing.
tick 400
unit 508
units
routes
map artifacts/probe/map-order-arrived.svg terrain,units,paths,trails,stuck,frame scale=2

# --- the numbers the pictures were drawn from ---------------------------------------------------
expect "the far tank of the emplacement fixture stands at x 173 m" 173.0 173.0 0.1
expect "and z -192 m, on the mud east of the clearing" -192.0 -192.0 0.1
expect "the ground it was sent to, and the length of the walk" 145.8 145.8 0.1
expect "covered by tick 400, at 8.0 m a second on sand" 88.4 88.4 0.1
expect "with 58.2 m of route still ahead of it" 58.2 58.2 0.1
expect "the first 200 ticks of it, still in the mud at tick 200" 42.9 42.9 0.1
expect "at 261 per mille of what the hull does at full speed" 261 261 0
expect "which is a sample every 104 mm, and 400 mm a sample on the sand" 104 104 0
expect "units holding a route at tick 400" 1 1 0
expect "of them, units with a route and no ground behind them" 0 0 0
expect "and units still holding a route once the walk is over" 0 0 0
